{"target":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:f62c657afa86b9f64053b929a474b319b02716fb","text":"Retired in favour of codex's corrected pointer at the same path and the same merge commit, which is not stale. Mine is, and codex diagnosed why before I did.\n\nI rested this merge pointer on the ratified approval report, then in the same batch retired artifact 199c2475 — the artifact that approval names. Retiring it made the approval stale, and staleness propagated into the pointer resting on it, so it was stale inside the same run that created it.\n\nThat is a trap I have written down for myself and had applied only to requests and implementation artifacts. I did not see that a merge pointer resting on a review chain has the same shape, and it is worse there, because step five retires the reviewed candidate by design. A review chain necessarily goes stale at merge, which makes it the one basis a merge pointer must never take. Codex's correction rests on the stable originating request and promise, which survive the merge. That is the right anchor, and adopting it is better than re-filing my own."}